@node CMacs AI Harness
@chapter CMacs AI Harness: an Agent in a Buffer

@cindex AI harness
@cindex agentic harness
@cindex transcript

@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-harness} opens an agentic AI session in a buffer: a
transcript the library owns, rendered natively, with an editable prompt
at the end.

It is @emph{not} @code{cmacs-ai-chat}, and the difference is worth
stating because the two look similar from outside.  A chat buffer is an
Org document you can edit and save; a harness buffer is a rendered view
of a transcript ai-glib owns, redrawn from its blocks.  A chat is a
conversation you keep; a harness is an agent you are driving.  That is
also why exporting to Org exists here and not there --- the buffer is
not Org to begin with.

@section The three entry points

@table @code
@item cmacs-ai-harness
Asks nothing.  Uses @code{cmacs-ai-harness-provider},
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-model} and @code{default-directory}.
@item cmacs-ai-harness-with-provider
Asks for a provider, then a model.
@item cmacs-ai-harness-with-provider-in-directory
Asks for a provider, a directory, then a model.
@end table

The directory is not cosmetic.  @file{CLAUDE.md}, @file{.claude}, the
project's own command files and every relative path a tool touches
resolve against it --- an agent started in the wrong directory is a
different agent.

@section Keys

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Key @tab Does
@item @kbd{C-c C-c} @tab send the prompt
@item @kbd{C-c C-k} @tab cancel the run, or clear the prompt when idle
@item @kbd{C-c C-g} @tab write the prompt in a dedicated buffer
@item @kbd{C-c C-e} @tab export the session as Org
@item @kbd{C-c E} @tab export the session as markdown
@item @kbd{C-c C-t} @tab expand or collapse the block at point
@item @kbd{C-c C-p} @tab switch provider, carrying the old CLI's own history
@item @kbd{C-c C-r} @tab reset: a genuinely fresh session on the same provider
@item @kbd{C-c C-n} @tab show what the wrapped CLI remembers
@item @kbd{C-c C-u} @tab ask the provider what it has been spending
@item @kbd{TAB} @tab complete a @code{/command} or an @code{@@path}
@end multitable

@kbd{C-c C-k} is one key for two things because that is what the
terminal harness's @kbd{^C} does, and the two are never ambiguous:
there is either a run to stop or there is not.

@subsection The compose buffer

@kbd{C-c C-g} opens a compose buffer seeded with whatever is already in
the prompt, so reaching for a real buffer part-way through a long prompt
does not lose it.  @kbd{C-c C-c} there installs the text as the prompt
and closes the buffer; @kbd{C-c C-k} discards it.

The terminal harness hands the prompt to @env{$EDITOR} for this.  Inside
Emacs that would be an editor inside an editor, so it is just a buffer.

@section Export

@kbd{C-c C-e} (Org) and @kbd{C-c E} (markdown) prompt with
@code{read-file-name}, so it
is the same directory browsing and completion as @kbd{C-x C-f} rather
than a bespoke picker.  The default name mirrors
@code{cmacs-ai-chat-save-name-format} with @samp{harness} in it:

@example
260816-225824-harness-ollama.org
@end example

controlled by @code{cmacs-ai-harness-export-name-format}, under
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-dir}.  The extension comes from ai-glib, so an
Org export and @command{ai-tui}'s @code{/export org} cannot disagree
about what to call it.

Three things about the export are ai-glib's doing and worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Blocks export expanded whatever the buffer shows.}  A collapsed
tool group renders as one summary line, and a file recording ``Edited 3
files'' without saying which is not a record of anything.
@item
@strong{The rendering is not the content.}  The @samp{>} markers and the
@samp{✳ thinking ⌄} chrome orient a reader on screen and are noise in a
file --- in Org, @samp{>} is not even syntax.  Prose blocks export their
own text; tool and todo blocks keep their markers, because there
@samp{✓} and @samp{+21-6} @emph{are} the information.
@item
@strong{Prose passes through, output is fenced.}  Models already write
markdown, so escaping an answer would ruin every code fence in it.
@end itemize

@section The AI menu

Right-click (or @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick}) offers a @strong{Harness}
group in these buffers: write this prompt for me, summarize this
session, next steps from this session, explain this block, copy this
block, and export as markdown or Org.

``Write this prompt for me'' does two different things on purpose.  With
an empty prompt it asks what you want and drafts one.  With a draft
already there it strengthens what you wrote without broadening the
scope --- replacing your intent with its own would make the feature
worse than typing it yourself.

``Summarize this session'' reads the @emph{exported} transcript rather
than the buffer text, so it can say what the agent actually did and not
only what it narrated.

@section Session control

ai-glib grew mid-flight provider switching, native-history import,
session reset and usage reporting at the same time as @command{ai-tui}
grew a panel system, a theme picker and an animation loop.  The
capabilities are worth having; the chrome is not, because Emacs already
has better versions of all of it --- a buffer @emph{is} a panel,
@code{customize-face} @emph{is} a theme picker, and nothing here needs to
animate.  Each of these is an ordinary command that prints an ordinary
message, and every result lands in an ordinary buffer.

@subsection Switching provider mid-session

@kbd{C-c C-p} moves the conversation to another provider.  The
transcript on screen, the message history behind it, the system prompt
and the working directory all stay; only who answers the next turn
changes.  It is refused while a turn is in flight, and refused if the
incoming provider cannot accept the tool endpoint this session is
using---in which case the current provider is left untouched rather than
half-swapped.

The interesting part is what a CLI leaves behind.  A wrapped coding
agent keeps a transcript of its own that this buffer never sees: the
blocks here are what the library observed, and the agent remembers a
great deal more.  With @code{cmacs-ai-harness-import-native-context} on
(the default), the switch reads that transcript first and hands it to
the incoming provider as context, so the conversation survives the move.
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-show-carried-context} shows what was carried.

Trimming to @code{cmacs-ai-harness-native-context-limit} keeps the
@emph{end} of the transcript---the recent turns---and says so in the
text it hands over, because a model given a silently shortened history
cannot tell anything is missing.

This build reads @code{claude-code}, @code{claude-tmux},
@code{codex-cli} and @code{grok-build}.  The others keep history in
SQLite and are deliberately @strong{not} half-read: a partial import is
invisible to the model that receives it.

@subsection Reset is not clear

@kbd{C-c C-r} and a cleared buffer are different operations.

@code{/clear} empties the transcript this buffer renders.  A wrapped CLI
keeps its @emph{own} session id, so after a clear it carries on resuming
the conversation you thought you had thrown away---the buffer looks
empty and the agent does not.

@kbd{C-c C-r} drops that session id, turns off
@code{continue-session}, and builds a new conversation.  That also
forgets tool approvals, todos and agent results, and gives the session a
new executor---so tools are re-wired afterwards, which the command does
for you.  Provider, model, system prompt and working directory are kept.

@subsection What the CLI remembers

@kbd{C-c C-n} shows the wrapped CLI's own transcript: which file it
lives in, how many messages survived the last compaction, how many were
dropped before that boundary, and the text itself.  It answers ``what
would I be handing over'' before you hand it over.

@subsection Usage

@kbd{C-c C-u} asks the provider what it has been spending;
@kbd{C-u C-c C-u} asks for past periods instead of the current quota
windows.

The answer arrives in its own buffer when the provider replies, and
@strong{not} as a return value.  The query spawns the CLI as a JSON-RPC
peer and can sit there for half a minute.  Blocking Emacs for that would
be bad on its own; under @option{--gowl} Emacs is also the compositor,
so it would freeze the desktop rather than merely the editor.  Only some
CLIs report at all---codex and grok do.

@subsection Deadlines

A wrapped CLI gets thirty minutes for one run, ai-glib's default, so a
CLI wedged on a half-open socket cannot pin the session forever.
ai-glib's own @command{ai} and @command{ai-tui} disable it outright, on
the grounds that a long agentic run legitimately outlives any deadline
you would pick and being killed mid-edit is worse than waiting.  Set
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-process-timeout} to 0 for the same behaviour
here.

@section Tools

A harness has cmacs's whole MCP tool surface---buffers, files, project
search, @code{apropos}, @code{describe-function}---on top of ai-glib's
own @code{bash}, @code{read}, @code{write}, @code{edit}, @code{glob} and
@code{grep}.  Roughly sixty tools, filtered by the three
@code{cmacs-ai-mcp-bridge-*} knobs @code{cmacs-ai-chat} already uses.

@code{/tools} lists them; @code{/todos} shows the agent's todo list.

@subsection Two mechanisms, chosen by provider

They are genuinely different, and ai-glib refuses to pretend otherwise.

An @strong{HTTP model}---claude, openai, gemini, grok, ollama,
@code{openai-compatible} and any endpoint named in
@code{cmacs-ai-openai-compatible-endpoints}---is sent a @code{tools}
array built from the conversation's @code{AiToolExecutor}.  cmacs adopts that executor into
its own handle registry, registers the MCP bridge on it, and sets
@code{local-tools}.

A @strong{command-line agent} runs its own tools in its own process and
ignores that array entirely---@code{ai_conversation_set_local_tools}
silently refuses one.  It is handed an @strong{MCP config} naming this
cmacs instead: @code{cmacs-brigade-host-provision} mints a random token
and a 0600 file under @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR} describing @command{emacs
--mcp-relay}, and the agent connects back.  Codex is the one CLI ai-glib
declares no MCP endpoint kind for, so a codex harness gets no cmacs
tools; the brigade's own @code{codex} worker delivers them through a
@env{CODEX_HOME} overlay instead.

The mode line says which is in play, and shows a warning when wiring
failed.  A provider that silently took no tools is otherwise invisible
from the transcript.

@subsection Codex takes an overlay, not a config file

Every other CLI agent is handed a path: claude and opencode read a
config file named on the command line, grok takes an overlay
@env{GROK_HOME} from ai-glib.  Codex reads
@file{CODEX_HOME/config.toml} and has @strong{no flag naming another
file}, so ai-glib's codex client declares no config endpoint at all and
@code{cmacs-ai-harness-set-mcp-config} refuses it.

Delivery is therefore an overlay home: a 0700 directory holding our
@file{config.toml}---the user's own settings first, our server table
appended---with every other entry of the real @file{~/.codex} symlinked
in.  @file{auth.json} above all, or the run arrives unauthenticated and
the failure reads as a model error.  @env{CODEX_HOME} is then set on the
session with @code{cmacs-ai-harness-set-env}.

The @option{-c mcp_servers...} override would have avoided the directory
entirely and is deliberately not used: it puts the socket path and the
token in argv, which is world-readable through @file{/proc}.

This is the same overlay the brigade's own @code{codex} worker builds,
and the same function---one implementation of the symlink care that
makes an overlay usable at all.

@subsection Dialects

The agents do not agree on a config schema.
@code{cmacs-brigade-host-provision} takes a @code{:format}---@code{claude},
@code{opencode} or @code{grok}---and emits the right one; the provider
decides how the file is delivered (a @option{--mcp-config} flag,
@env{OPENCODE_CONFIG}, an overlay @env{GROK_HOME}).

For grok the server is named @code{cmacs-brigade} rather than
@code{cmacs}: grok scopes MCP servers by name across config sources, and
a name a project's own @file{.mcp.json} already uses is judged repo-local
and never started in an untrusted folder.

@subsection When an agent says it has no tools

The mode line is the first thing to read---a tool count, @code{mcp}, or a
warning when wiring failed.  After that, @file{*Messages*} carries the
reason: the wiring says so out loud rather than failing quietly, because
a session that looks tool-enabled and has none is invisible from the
transcript.

For a CLI agent, that agent's own diagnostics are the authority on
whether the server actually started:

@example
GROK_HOME=<overlay> grok mcp doctor cmacs-brigade --json
@end example

@command{grok mcp list} is @emph{not} a useful check---it reads the
config file without applying grok's project-scope merge or its trust
rule, so it prints servers that will never start.

@subsection What a CLI agent may call

@code{cmacs-ai-harness-cli-tool-allowlist} defaults to @code{*}, matching
the chat buffer.

A harness buffer is a person typing a prompt and watching every tool call
render in front of them---the trust level of @kbd{M-x eval-expression},
not of an unattended worker running against a budget.  Withholding
@code{eval} from an agent you are driving turn by turn buys nothing and
produces exactly the failure this feature fixes: an agent narrating tool
calls it cannot make.  Set
@code{cmacs-brigade-restrict-privileged-tools} to @code{t} to hold
@code{eval}, @code{bash} and the C-patching tools back from @code{*}.

@subsection Adding your own

@lisp
(add-hook 'cmacs-ai-harness-executor-functions
          (lambda (executor _provider)
            (cmacs-ai-tools-register executor "my_tool" "..." params #'fn)))
@end lisp

A @emph{sibling} of @code{cmacs-ai-chat-executor-functions}, not the same
hook: that one's members were written for chat buffers and include the
brigade's agent-spawning tools, so running them here would silently
change what every existing member is handed.

@subsection Exports carry tool output

A transcript exported with @kbd{C-c C-e} includes tool blocks
verbatim---command output, file contents, MCP results.  Worth knowing
before sharing one.

@section How it works

The grouping, the summarising and the input pipeline all live in
ai-glib.  This buffer knows nothing about what a tool is, exactly as
@command{ai-tui} does not.

@subsection Why a C bridge and not cmacs-gi

ai-glib's own documentation shows this frontend written in Elisp over
GObject Introspection.  That does not work here yet: cmacs-gi skips
out-parameters entirely and has no signal support, and the harness needs
both.  @code{ai_rendered_text_get_span()} and
@code{ai_completion_result_get_item_fields()} are out-parameter APIs
@emph{because} a plain struct behind a pointer does not survive
@command{g-ir-scanner}, and @code{::block-changed} is what makes
streaming appear at all.

Teaching cmacs-gi those two things is worth doing and is a bigger,
riskier change to a subsystem everything else already depends on.  So
@file{cmacs/ai/cmacs-ai-harness.c} is a purpose-built bridge, in the
shape @file{cmacs-ai-stream.c} already uses.

@subsection Two things a frontend gets wrong

Both are handled in one place here, and both are silent when wrong:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Span offsets are byte offsets into UTF-8.}
@code{byte-to-position} converts.  Skip it and every face after the
first accented filename lands somewhere else.
@item
@strong{@code{::block-changed} is not optional.}  Streaming mutates a
block in place, which the insertion signal does not report.  Without it
the buffer shows the first delta of each reply and then stops.
@end enumerate

@subsection Evil, and why the mode is not a special-mode

The obvious base class for a buffer with a read-only transcript is
@code{special-mode}, and it is the wrong one.  Two things follow from it
and both are wrong here: the buffer becomes read-only outright, and
@strong{Evil gives modes derived from @code{special-mode} normal state}
--- so under Doom every letter you type at the prompt is a motion or an
operator, and the buffer looks broken rather than modal.

So the mode derives from @code{fundamental-mode}, the transcript is
protected by a @code{read-only} text property covering exactly the part
that needs it, and the buffer opens in @emph{insert} state --- the same
treatment eshell, vterm and a commit message get, because the thing you
do here is type.  @kbd{ESC} still reaches normal state for reading back
through the transcript.

The @code{evil-set-initial-state} call is inside
@code{with-eval-after-load}, not behind a bare @code{fboundp} guard as
some other cmacs modes use: that guard silently does nothing when the
file loads before Evil, and the failure mode is landing in normal state,
which does not look like an Evil problem when you hit it.

@subsection Both stickiness flags on the read-only property

@code{cmacs-ai-harness--read-only-props} is @code{(read-only t
front-sticky t rear-nonsticky t)} and neither flag is optional.

@code{rear-nonsticky} is what lets the first character of the prompt be
typed at all: without it the property is inherited by whatever is
inserted after the separator, and the buffer cannot be typed in.

@code{front-sticky} is what makes the run unmodifiable in the
@emph{middle}.  With @code{rear-nonsticky} alone --- the comint idiom,
and what this used to be --- Emacs decides inserted text would join
neither neighbouring interval and permits the insertion, so only the
run's edges were protected: the prompt glyph and every transcript block
could be typed into.

The test asserts it by inserting mid-run, using the constant rather than
a copy of its value --- a literal in the test would assert how Emacs
behaves rather than how the harness marks its blocks, and would keep
passing after someone dropped @code{front-sticky} from the real one.

@subsection Block ids, not positions

@code{cmacs-ai-harness-block-at} reports an @code{:id} unique for the
life of the process.  The buffer's region map is keyed on it, because
positions shift when blocks are removed and ids do not.

@section Configuration

@multitable @columnfractions .4 .6
@headitem Variable @tab Means
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-provider} @tab provider symbol, or the configured default
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-model} @tab model, or the provider's own default
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-dir} @tab where exports are offered first
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-export-name-format} @tab export file name, without extension
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-prompt} @tab the prompt marker
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-enable-tools} @tab give the agent cmacs's tools
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-system-prompt} @tab instructions, or nil
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-cli-tool-allowlist} @tab what a CLI agent may call
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-default-directory-function} @tab where a new harness runs
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-executor-functions} @tab hook, @code{(EXECUTOR PROVIDER)}
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-run-chat-executor-functions} @tab also run the chat hook
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-import-native-context} @tab carry a CLI's own history across a provider switch
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-native-context-limit} @tab byte ceiling on what is carried
@item @code{cmacs-ai-harness-process-timeout} @tab ms for one CLI run; 0 removes the deadline
@end multitable

Faces are one per ai-glib style role.  The library's tags are
@strong{roles, not colours}: @code{added} says ``this run is an
addition'' and says nothing about green, so the mapping is cmacs's and a
theme can override any of it.
